      <h2>The Art of a Balanced Plate</h2>

      <article>
        <h3>The Power of Well-Rounded Meals</h3>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1490645935967-10de6ba17061?w=300&h=200&fit=crop"
            alt="A freshly prepared colorful salad bowl with avocado, eggs, and greens"
            width="300"
            height="200"
          >
          <figcaption>
            A vibrant, well-rounded meal provides sustained physical stamina and mental focus.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Distributing your food groups evenly ensures your mind and body get clean energy to get through the school day. 
          Prioritizing a mix of leafy greens, complex carbohydrates, and clean proteins keeps you feeling full and alert.
        </p>

        <p>
          Check out alternative meal ideas here:
          <a href="https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/nutritionsource/healthy-eating-plate/" target="_blank">
            Harvard Healthy Eating Plate
          </a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Smarter Snacking for Energy</h3>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1464965911861-746a04b4bca6?w=300&h=200&fit=crop"
            alt="A selection of raw almonds, walnuts, and mixed fresh berries"
            width="300"
            height="200"
          >
          <figcaption>
            Nutrient-dense options like raw nuts and fresh berries prevent afternoon fatigue.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Snacking is highly beneficial when you swap processed chips and sugary sodas for whole foods. 
          Grabbing a handful of pumpkin seeds, sliced fruit, or Greek yogurt offers energy without the typical sugar crash.
        </p>

        <p>
          Discover foundational diet facts here:
          <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/nutrition/index.html" target="_blank">
            CDC Nutrition Resources
          </a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section id="nutrition-tips">
      <h2>Essential Wellness Tips for Students</h2>

      <article>
        <h3>Prioritize Daily Hydration</h3>

        <p>
          Drinking adequate amounts of water maximizes brain concentration, aids digestion, and keeps skin healthy. 
          Try carrying a reusable water container to class to help build a consistent sipping habit.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Eat From the Rainbow</h3>

        <p>
          The bright colors found in different fruits and vegetables indicate distinct vitamins and protective minerals. 
          Aiming for a colorful layout across your meals naturally ensures a highly nutritious diet.
        </p>

        <p>
          Read up on healthy food selections here:
          <a href="https://www.nutrition.gov/" target="_blank">
            Nutrition.gov Resources
          </a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <aside>
      <h2>Quick Nutrition Note</h2>

      <div>
        <p>
          Try utilizing the 50-25-25 rule: dedicate half of your meal container to colorful produce, 
          one quarter to whole grains, and the final quarter to a solid protein source.
        </p>

